#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da1c47f5-5e7e-50ce-b14e-b9f3f3f757ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da1c47f5-5e7e-50ce-b14e-b9f3f3f757ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320054} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc4fe866-71a8-5d45-8c02-73304c3706b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50520627-69a0-5672-92d6-61584ad60ba6" :question/math-academy-id "q-352870" :question/problem "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [1,2].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}},$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{8}(2)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(2)^{2}}]-[\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4(1)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{16}-(-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f291e03-abb0-5fe4-a583-4a48393d5666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392737} {:answer/id #uuid "83980ef2-3253-5c8c-b7ac-9bf91e820d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392738} {:answer/id #uuid "16e21676-9e8e-5cda-891a-9e3a99ad370a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392739} {:answer/id #uuid "c65e70ed-792a-59d7-8500-3fca29572bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392740} {:answer/id #uuid "301b975b-e18b-51b6-9517-6f4e286dee3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "301b975b-e18b-51b6-9517-6f4e286dee3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392741} :answer-field/id #uuid "aca7ae0e-ab85-5150-9d28-8959d835e214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2c7c9a6-db2e-5889-8918-df15ae7908cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-51267" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb30925ffaca0ce59de7508e0f3710fbb26f3a80dfbd80b689e9ee5a4cf7d699.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}$ for $x\\in [-3,4],$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $a=-3,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2x}{4}=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "385be674-f982-5b0d-a246-7c05ff2eae39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440944} {:answer/id #uuid "4e80d767-11f6-5557-956d-f161a62d1488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440946} {:answer/id #uuid "628f8af6-109d-5b4d-ba07-98c8c66ae500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440947} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a976e6-bf11-50a3-a22a-0fbafda71f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440951} {:answer/id #uuid "d8b57b3f-c7b4-5a3a-a497-6401eb5b176f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e80d767-11f6-5557-956d-f161a62d1488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440946} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9178dda-3c7f-55ff-93f7-642762e083ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7f022af-185f-5ff9-8180-e7c6bb598640" :question/math-academy-id "q-82640" :question/problem "The length of the curve $y={cos}^{2}⁡x$ on $[0,\\pi ]$ is given by" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x,$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\pi .$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3282f6dd-ea0d-5250-b6a0-e1760944dcbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440943} {:answer/id #uuid "4c99b7f5-f882-59fc-acc5-121343a55257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440945} {:answer/id #uuid "b82a6116-d805-5f63-89ef-e785b5a2ca78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440952} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ec2b73-e05f-5a1d-a896-08fa4c87c85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440955} {:answer/id #uuid "fa7cfda3-585f-5143-8a02-9cd0f064fb95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3282f6dd-ea0d-5250-b6a0-e1760944dcbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440943} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f3f0bc6-2b44-5994-b979-b5f7080dc894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6cce7317-466a-565b-96aa-fdea6ef81a13" :question/math-academy-id "q-85595" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula:\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}{x}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\sqrt{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\sqrt{2x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[\\sqrt{2x}]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1+2x.$ Then, $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 4 \\\\ u & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({9}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27-1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135fa4af-c45b-5bcb-a835-dbd3b68255f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440942} {:answer/id #uuid "6fade50c-3a34-568e-84e3-7d56cc0c5b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440948} {:answer/id #uuid "874efc84-b50d-5b16-b1a0-a3259348480c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440949} {:answer/id #uuid "87d8988f-ee76-5568-a61f-b92eb15fbc84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440950} {:answer/id #uuid "c78a19eb-3b4d-5b88-9309-0ea8e68d379a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fade50c-3a34-568e-84e3-7d56cc0c5b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440948} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f3c91bd-b004-5988-9785-3182aa9872f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4cc4c19-d62f-5fe8-890e-23b350c71bdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-85663" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [1,4].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)^{3/2},$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{1/2}=\\sqrt{x-1}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\sqrt{x-1})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+x-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}]|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(8-1)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
